1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
They're the cities of the sea and the power players of the ports.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Bigger does not mean stronger.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Some know how to water ski, while others look right out of science fiction.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Nobody has done anything similar.

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:21,000
A few carry enough frigid energy to heat 30,000 homes for a year.

6
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Some are as sophisticated as they are practical.

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
This is really a truly amazing piece of machinery.

8
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:33,660
machinery. And one will even flip you out. Now, Super Ships on Modern Martins.

9
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:48,060
What does it mean to be super?

10
00:00:48,061 --> 00:01:02,061
In the world of ships, it could be gargantuan size, immense power, cutting-edge technology, or the ability to excite our imagination.

11
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:12,200
When it comes to speed, multi-hulled ships are definitely super.

12
00:01:12,201 --> 00:01:16,800
Ships having more than a single hull tend to reach higher speeds

13
00:01:16,801 --> 00:01:21,800
due to a lighter weight and less surface contact with the water than single-hulled ships.

14
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:28,000
Their wider beam also gives them better stability on the ocean.

15
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:34,220
Take the CAT, the fastest car ferry in North America.

16
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:40,560
CAT is a high-speed twin-hull catamaran-style ferry,

17
00:01:40,561 --> 00:01:43,160
one of the largest of this kind of ship in the world.

18
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:48,180
The Kent runs seasonal routes between Portland or Bar Harbor, Maine,

19
00:01:48,740 --> 00:01:50,040
and Yarmouth, Nova Scotia.

20
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:54,680
Its travel time is about half that of traditional ferries.

21
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:58,360
You never really get used to the power that's involved here

22
00:01:58,361 --> 00:02:00,120
when you're coming up to speed

23
00:02:00,121 --> 00:02:04,020
and knowing that you've got a deckload of 200 vehicles,

24
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:06,380
including buses and motorhomes,

25
00:02:06,381 --> 00:02:10,980
and you can get up to 40 knots in probably two minutes.

26
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:12,620
It's still quite a feeling.

27
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:18,700
The ship is 98 meters in length, a bit longer than a football field,

28
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:24,740
and 27 meters in width, so a little bit narrower than a football field.

29
00:02:24,741 --> 00:02:30,740
She carries 220 cars or so and upwards of 900 passengers.

30
00:02:32,220 --> 00:02:36,080
The ship's aluminum hulls and superstructure allow it to be strong,

31
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:38,880
yet lighter on the water.

32
00:02:38,881 --> 00:02:40,920
And that's the whole idea of these vessels,

33
00:02:40,921 --> 00:02:44,380
is to keep them lightweight with a lot of power.

34
00:02:44,381 --> 00:02:47,420
More than 38,000 horsepower roar from the CAT's four

35
00:02:47,421 --> 00:02:50,460
lightweight high-speed turbocharged engines,

36
00:02:50,461 --> 00:02:55,500
located within the vessel's two narrow hulls.

37
00:02:55,501 --> 00:02:58,700
Where we're standing now is roughly close to four meters

38
00:02:58,701 --> 00:03:01,740
across, and this is the widest part of the hull.

39
00:03:01,741 --> 00:03:05,300
So that gives you some idea how little is actually in the water.

40
00:03:05,301 --> 00:03:08,880
The maximum depth of this is 3.4 meters.

41
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:14,360
Most ships are, you know, probably 12, 13, 14 meters, some 18 meters.

42
00:03:14,980 --> 00:03:15,900
So it's not very much.

43
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:21,860
As the slender hulls meet a wave, they pierce through rather than over it.

44
00:03:22,780 --> 00:03:26,920
This tendency helps stabilize the ship, keeping it level in the water.

45
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:32,120
While the design gives the ship better hydrodynamics,

46
00:03:32,121 --> 00:03:37,120
It's up to the engines to power it across the Gulf of Maine.

47
00:03:37,121 --> 00:03:43,120
From the bridge, the crew monitors and controls the engines and their corresponding jets.

48
00:03:43,121 --> 00:03:49,620
Basically it's the exact same principle as a jet ski, but just on a much larger scale

49
00:03:49,621 --> 00:03:51,620
with much more power.

50
00:03:51,621 --> 00:03:58,420
The water is taken in through an inlet and put through an impeller, and that water goes

51
00:03:58,421 --> 00:04:01,840
through a stator bolt which takes the twist out of the water and shoots it back

52
00:04:01,841 --> 00:04:05,740
out through the nozzle and that's what provides all the thrust that gives the

53
00:04:05,741 --> 00:04:13,440
ship its power at top speed the four jets shoot water through at over 4,700

54
00:04:13,441 --> 00:04:19,700
gallons per second at that rate they could fill an average backyard swimming

55
00:04:19,701 --> 00:04:27,340
pool in five seconds the powerful jets of water not only propel but also steer

56
00:04:27,341 --> 00:04:28,727
this super ship.

57
00:04:28,728 --> 00:04:35,660
Any side motion or turning around is all done using the jets by just angling the

58
00:04:35,661 --> 00:04:38,482
nozzles in the directions you want.

59
00:04:38,483 --> 00:04:43,320
The steering wheel on this 826 ton vessel looks more like it

60
00:04:43,321 --> 00:04:46,178
belongs on a toy. She's very maneuverable.

61
00:04:46,179 --> 00:04:49,580
She responds very quickly, a lot better than most,

62
00:04:49,820 --> 00:04:54,386
you know, almost like a speedboat. Visually, the ship is spectacular.

63
00:04:54,387 --> 00:04:56,040
The shape of the hulls of

64
00:04:56,041 --> 00:05:03,480
the ship are of course designed to permit its speed but once you see are underway at sea you

65
00:05:03,481 --> 00:05:11,260
realize what a unique vessel it truly is in cat the ship's builder has been building fast catamaran

66
00:05:11,261 --> 00:05:18,520
ships in Australia since 1984 today they apply their unique designs to commercial and military

67
00:05:18,521 --> 00:05:25,860
vessels around the world some are even bigger than the cat but still able to cruise at up to

68
00:05:25,861 --> 00:05:31,331
40 knots. And what happens when you add another hull to a catamaran?

69
00:05:31,332 --> 00:05:32,860
You get a trimaran.

70
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:42,300
In 2008, a super trimaran called the Earth Race set a world record for a powerboat circling

71
00:05:42,301 --> 00:05:45,685
the Earth. 60 days, 23 hours and 49 minutes.

72
00:05:45,686 --> 00:05:49,300
Almost 14 days faster than the previous record.

73
00:05:49,301 --> 00:05:50,001
record.

74
00:05:50,002 --> 00:05:55,440
Built specifically to achieve the record, the hulls are able to pierce waves,

75
00:05:55,980 --> 00:06:01,480
and the entire trimaran can go up to 23 feet underwater for a few seconds at a time.

76
00:06:03,460 --> 00:06:10,560
The 78-foot-long, 24-foot-wide, super-light carbon Kevlar composite vessel is strong enough

77
00:06:10,561 --> 00:06:18,520
to handle waves up to 50 feet high. Not only that, but it's powered with biodiesel,

78
00:06:18,521 --> 00:06:20,740
and has a zero carbon footprint.

79
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:27,340
Another one-of-a-kind vessel looks more like a huge buoy than a ship,

80
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:31,500
and its super status is all in its name, the Flip.

81
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:37,740
It's owned by the U.S. Navy, but operated for acoustic research

82
00:06:37,741 --> 00:06:39,780
by the Scripps Institution of Oceanography

83
00:06:39,781 --> 00:06:42,400
at the University of California, San Diego.

84
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:46,240
The Flip has been around for several decades,

85
00:06:46,620 --> 00:06:48,380
but has never been replicated.

86
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:53,520
This is the Navy Research Platform FLIP.

87
00:06:53,521 --> 00:06:57,520
Now FLIP stands for Floating Instrument Platform.

88
00:06:57,521 --> 00:07:00,520
Researchers here at the Scripps Institution of Oceanography

89
00:07:00,521 --> 00:07:03,520
developed the concept for a floating instrument platform

90
00:07:03,521 --> 00:07:07,520
to minimize its movement when in the vertical at sea.

91
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:11,520
Because of its very small waterline

92
00:07:11,521 --> 00:07:15,520
and the mass of its structure below the surface of the water,

93
00:07:15,521 --> 00:07:20,300
A FLIP will have a draft of 300 feet when it's in the vertical.

94
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:24,260
It moves less than one-tenth of wave height.

95
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:28,040
It stands perfectly still in the ocean,

96
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:32,820
not subject to the same motions that a conventional vessel would have.

97
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:37,200
Therefore, for acoustic studies, it is a very valuable platform.

98
00:07:39,740 --> 00:07:43,680
That's because a stable FLIP means stable monitoring equipment

99
00:07:43,681 --> 00:07:46,280
and more precise measurements for the scientists.

100
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:53,220
When FLIP goes to sea to support an oceanographic research project,

101
00:07:53,580 --> 00:07:56,920
the project will normally last three to four weeks.

102
00:07:57,640 --> 00:08:00,800
During that time, three equipment deployment booms

103
00:08:00,801 --> 00:08:05,280
allow scientists to get even clearer acoustic signals 60 feet away from FLIP,

104
00:08:06,120 --> 00:08:07,820
without noise interference from the vessel.

105
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:11,520
Since FLIP is a non-propelled vessel,

106
00:08:11,521 --> 00:08:15,760
For research missions, a Navy tug will usually tow-flip out to sea.

107
00:08:17,620 --> 00:08:20,380
Until then, flip docks in its horizontal position.

108
00:08:22,340 --> 00:08:26,520
So when we flip, this thing that will be submerged will be on the water,

109
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:31,240
all the way up to that red pipe you see all the way on the front of the flip.

110
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:35,580
When in the horizontal, the ballast tanks are filled with air.

111
00:08:36,120 --> 00:08:41,200
To conduct the flipping process, we vent that air.

112
00:08:41,201 --> 00:08:44,800
And as the air escapes, it's replaced by seawater.

113
00:08:44,900 --> 00:08:49,400
This changes the displacement of the after end of FLIP,

114
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:52,740
causing it to weigh more than the amount of water it displaces.

115
00:08:55,540 --> 00:08:58,120
The 55 feet above the surface water course

116
00:08:58,220 --> 00:09:01,960
is the inhabited area where the crew and science party

117
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:02,760
do their work.

118
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:10,000
During the roughly 27-minute transition,

119
00:09:10,001 --> 00:09:13,000
Everybody stays outside on the deck.

120
00:09:13,001 --> 00:09:16,300
The people that come out with us many, many times,

121
00:09:16,301 --> 00:09:20,720
they like to lay down on the deck on horizontal.

122
00:09:20,721 --> 00:09:27,140
When the flip goes vertical, they find themselves vertical also.

123
00:09:27,141 --> 00:09:32,060
Flipping an entire vessel means having an extremely versatile interior.

124
00:09:32,061 --> 00:09:37,400
On flip, some of the unusual things you will see are the two heads.

125
00:09:37,401 --> 00:09:41,200
There is a sink arrangement, rather unique.

126
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:46,640
There is the commode, which rotates 90 degrees so that it's usable.

127
00:09:46,680 --> 00:09:48,240
And the shower.

128
00:09:48,280 --> 00:09:53,080
The shower in this head, of course, is for the vertical operation only.

129
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:55,680
Haven't learned to flip a shower yet.

130
00:09:55,720 --> 00:10:00,720
Even the engines that provide the ship's electricity are free to move.

131
00:10:00,760 --> 00:10:03,920
As with much of the other heavy equipment on board,

132
00:10:03,960 --> 00:10:06,560
the engines are mounted on trunnions.

133
00:10:06,561 --> 00:10:10,560
with the fuel tank included in the framework.

134
00:10:10,561 --> 00:10:13,560
The engine rotates freely.

135
00:10:13,561 --> 00:10:16,560
Trunnions are also responsible for rotating the generators,

136
00:10:16,561 --> 00:10:21,560
air compressors, bunks, even an entire galley.

137
00:10:21,561 --> 00:10:24,560
We're now entering the galley area on FLIP.

138
00:10:24,561 --> 00:10:29,560
This is a good example how the things that are located on trunnions operate.

139
00:10:29,561 --> 00:10:33,560
First of all, it's being held in position right now with a bolt

140
00:10:33,561 --> 00:10:37,400
bolt while Flip is in the horizontal.

141
00:10:37,401 --> 00:10:39,440
Now when you're ready to go to the vertical,

142
00:10:39,441 --> 00:10:44,080
the pin is removed to allow freedom of movement

143
00:10:44,081 --> 00:10:46,960
of all the galley equipment supported by trunnions,

144
00:10:46,961 --> 00:10:49,740
which allow rotational movement.

145
00:10:49,741 --> 00:10:52,760
While rotating equipment is odd enough,

146
00:10:52,761 --> 00:10:54,280
even the rooms go topsy-turvy.

147
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:01,720
We're now entering the main laboratory space onboard Flip.

148
00:11:01,721 --> 00:11:07,400
This is the compartment where the scientists will bring all their scientific equipment.

149
00:11:07,401 --> 00:11:12,220
You'll notice lights on the bulkhead now become the overhead lights.

150
00:11:12,221 --> 00:11:17,060
And above us, you see one of the work benches that will be used by scientists.

151
00:11:17,061 --> 00:11:23,260
Now, you see forward of us a six-man bunk room when Flip is in the vertical.

152
00:11:23,261 --> 00:11:28,100
The bunk room then is the uppermost space and normally referred to as the penthouse.

153
00:11:28,101 --> 00:11:31,820
Actually, like two different flips.

154
00:11:32,380 --> 00:11:34,880
This is the flip on horizontal, and we go vertical.

155
00:11:35,080 --> 00:11:36,720
It's a completely different thing.

156
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:38,540
It's like a day and night difference.

157
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:41,600
And when the scientist's work is done,

158
00:11:42,060 --> 00:11:44,460
the key to flipping back to the horizontal position

159
00:11:44,461 --> 00:11:47,040
is eight large compressed air bottles

160
00:11:47,041 --> 00:11:49,100
located in the ballast tanks of Flip.

161
00:11:51,240 --> 00:11:55,060
In about 20 minutes, air pressure forces the water out

162
00:11:55,061 --> 00:11:58,080
and allows Flip to return to its horizontal position.

163
00:11:58,100 --> 00:12:08,060
If you think Flip is impressive, how about the world's largest thermos, hauling enough energy to power a million homes for a week?

164
00:12:10,220 --> 00:12:16,300
The Flip has easily withstood waves of up to 80 feet during its more than 300 missions.

165
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:20,260
Super Ships will return on Modern Marvels.

166
00:12:20,261 --> 00:12:30,060
Super ships can be unique, blazingly fast, incredibly strong, or enormous.

167
00:12:31,780 --> 00:12:35,180
Container ships and oil tankers are some of the largest craft on the water.

168
00:12:36,980 --> 00:12:41,740
Yet, it's another type of super carrier that's among the fastest growing fleets of ships.

169
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:49,840
Liquefied natural gas, or LNG carriers, transport one of the world's most sought-after energy sources.

170
00:12:50,260 --> 00:12:54,900
It's a major part of the natural gas supply in many nations in the world,

171
00:12:55,020 --> 00:12:58,740
particularly in the Far East and Europe, and increasingly now in the United States.

172
00:12:59,380 --> 00:13:03,560
LNG is nothing more than natural gas that's been liquefied.

173
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:07,100
You reduce its temperature to minus 260 degrees Fahrenheit,

174
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:10,140
and when you do so, it turns from a gas to a liquid.

175
00:13:11,620 --> 00:13:15,280
Liquefaction reduces the volume of natural gas by a factor of 600,

176
00:13:15,281 --> 00:13:28,960
And that means the 900-foot-long, 141-foot-wide BW Suez Boston can haul a whole lot of natural gas at a speed of about 20 knots, or 23 miles per hour.

177
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:33,040
This ship holds 138,000 cubic meters of LNG.

178
00:13:33,920 --> 00:13:41,720
To the average person, that's enough gas to heat 30,000 homes for a year, or it would be enough to provide power for a million homes for a week.

179
00:13:41,721 --> 00:13:46,240
Natural gas in liquid form is neither flammable nor explosive.

180
00:13:47,860 --> 00:13:51,320
Although liquefaction makes the combustible natural gas stable,

181
00:13:51,960 --> 00:13:55,260
the possibility of a leak, or in a worst-case scenario,

182
00:13:55,460 --> 00:13:58,420
a terrorist attack on a ship, has some people worried.

183
00:13:59,420 --> 00:14:02,840
In the event that a plane or rocket pierced one of the ship's tanks,

184
00:14:04,460 --> 00:14:07,780
the LNG would not combust or explode like a bomb.

185
00:14:07,781 --> 00:14:15,760
instead it would be released and vaporized into gas if combustion occurred it would most likely

186
00:14:15,761 --> 00:14:21,640
affect only the area directly around the ship still the degree of concern has meant more

187
00:14:21,641 --> 00:14:29,380
stringent safety regulations for every LNG ship and terminal this includes raised security and

188
00:14:29,381 --> 00:14:36,380
US Coast Guard supervision of all LNG ships moving in or out of port. On the BW

189
00:14:36,381 --> 00:14:40,780
Suez Boston there's about eight feet of distance between the outer and inner

190
00:14:40,781 --> 00:14:47,140
hulls of the ship. Inside the inner hull are four containment tanks. Each of

191
00:14:47,141 --> 00:14:53,560
these has two walls. The tank holds the LNG itself. Insulation fills the space

192
00:14:53,561 --> 00:14:57,640
between the tanks two walls as well as the space between the tank and the inner

193
00:14:57,641 --> 00:14:58,341
hull.

194
00:14:58,342 --> 00:15:04,600
Nitrogen fills any gaps in the insulation and allows the ship to detect the most minute leak.

195
00:15:07,800 --> 00:15:11,800
And that would allow a ship to go to a terminal like this one, offload its cargo,

196
00:15:11,801 --> 00:15:17,720
and then go to a shipyard for repair. While the LNG carrier hulls frozen cargo,

197
00:15:17,721 --> 00:15:20,372
it does not itself use any refrigeration.

198
00:15:20,373 --> 00:15:23,800
The ship itself and the cargo tanks are actually just

199
00:15:23,801 --> 00:15:25,520
It's very well insulated.

200
00:15:25,521 --> 00:15:27,320
The best way to think about the ship

201
00:15:27,321 --> 00:15:31,080
is actually a very large floating thermos bottle.

202
00:15:31,081 --> 00:15:34,060
During their ocean journey from Trinidad to Boston,

203
00:15:34,061 --> 00:15:36,360
the LNG releases a small amount of vapor.

204
00:15:39,700 --> 00:15:42,440
This helps keep pressure from building in the tanks

205
00:15:42,441 --> 00:15:45,920
and maintains a low temperature overall.

206
00:15:45,921 --> 00:15:47,760
That's what we call boil off gas.

207
00:15:47,761 --> 00:15:49,460
On a ship like this, the boil off gas

208
00:15:49,461 --> 00:15:52,120
is actually used to propel the ship and run its engines.

209
00:15:52,121 --> 00:15:55,360
So it's actually a very, very efficient process.

210
00:15:55,361 --> 00:15:58,600
If for some reason the engine boil-off system fails,

211
00:15:58,601 --> 00:16:01,200
a backup system is in place.

212
00:16:01,201 --> 00:16:03,700
Behind me, you see these four green towers.

213
00:16:03,701 --> 00:16:06,260
And each of them is actually a vent mass associated

214
00:16:06,261 --> 00:16:08,740
with each of the four cargo tanks.

215
00:16:08,741 --> 00:16:12,140
Rather than let the pressure in the cargo tanks build up,

216
00:16:12,141 --> 00:16:14,640
these mass would act as safety relief valves

217
00:16:14,641 --> 00:16:17,340
and actually would relieve the boil-off gas

218
00:16:17,341 --> 00:16:20,880
and allow it to vent safely to the atmosphere.

219
00:16:20,881 --> 00:16:22,780
We're offloading the ship right now.

220
00:16:22,781 --> 00:16:25,780
Behind me you can see are what we call unloading arms.

221
00:16:25,781 --> 00:16:28,680
Once we connect the unloading arms to the ship's piping,

222
00:16:28,681 --> 00:16:33,560
the ship begins to pump the LNG cargo from its storage tanks

223
00:16:33,561 --> 00:16:35,260
into our storage tanks.

224
00:16:35,261 --> 00:16:38,260
While that's happening, we're simultaneously pumping the LNG

225
00:16:38,261 --> 00:16:41,040
out of our storage tanks and into our vaporizers,

226
00:16:41,041 --> 00:16:43,940
which regasifies the LNG and sends it directly

227
00:16:43,941 --> 00:16:45,300
to people's homes.

228
00:16:45,301 --> 00:16:48,400
From our terminal, we supply 20% of all the gas consumed

229
00:16:48,401 --> 00:16:51,160
in New England, and that's for power generation,

230
00:16:51,161 --> 00:16:53,080
for home heating, for commercial uses,

231
00:16:53,081 --> 00:16:56,280
industrial uses, everything.

232
00:16:56,281 --> 00:16:58,760
As demand for natural gas grows,

233
00:16:58,761 --> 00:17:01,640
so does the LNG fleet.

234
00:17:01,641 --> 00:17:05,240
Five or six years ago, there were roughly 100 or 120 LNG

235
00:17:05,241 --> 00:17:06,720
carriers in existence.

236
00:17:06,721 --> 00:17:09,260
Today, that number is slightly over 300.

237
00:17:09,261 --> 00:17:10,820
And as we project out in the future,

238
00:17:10,821 --> 00:17:14,220
there will be continued growth in the industry for sure.

239
00:17:14,221 --> 00:17:17,360
In the meantime, how do you count on safely maneuvering

240
00:17:17,361 --> 00:17:21,900
a mega LNG carrier in and out of a port,

241
00:17:21,901 --> 00:17:23,160
with super strength tugboats.

242
00:17:25,960 --> 00:17:29,180
They may be small compared to large cargo ships,

243
00:17:29,181 --> 00:17:31,140
but they make up for their size with power.

244
00:17:33,640 --> 00:17:37,180
While tugboats have been tugging for nearly 200 years,

245
00:17:37,181 --> 00:17:41,320
it wasn't until the 1920s, with the addition of diesel engines,

246
00:17:41,321 --> 00:17:44,620
that tugboats became the workhorses we know today.

247
00:17:44,621 --> 00:17:48,080
The mass-produced diesel engine really changed towboating.

248
00:17:48,280 --> 00:17:49,540
It extended the range.

249
00:17:49,720 --> 00:17:55,700
It was a lighter weight propulsion unit that allowed the tugs to have what we would call legs or more distance capability.

250
00:17:57,160 --> 00:18:00,860
Another 1920s tug addition were the rubber tire fenders.

251
00:18:02,060 --> 00:18:05,560
Today, tugboats still use recycled truck and airplane tires.

252
00:18:07,720 --> 00:18:11,380
Meanwhile, some tugboats have upped their towing ability with another innovation.

253
00:18:11,381 --> 00:18:16,380
Spectra rope, lighter yet stronger than any other rope in the world.

254
00:18:16,381 --> 00:18:25,380
One two-inch rope can handle up to 250,000 pounds of stress before tearing apart.

255
00:18:25,381 --> 00:18:30,380
Over the years, the basic types of tugboats have remained the same.

256
00:18:30,381 --> 00:18:36,380
Seagoing tugs are generally larger in order to better handle the open ocean.

257
00:18:36,381 --> 00:18:41,380
River tugs, or towboats, frequently maneuver barges up or downstream.

258
00:18:41,381 --> 00:18:45,380
And then there are the harbor tugboats.

259
00:18:45,381 --> 00:18:51,380
They're generally smaller and more maneuverable in order to handle narrow port channels.

260
00:18:51,381 --> 00:18:53,380
They're also incredibly strong.

261
00:18:53,381 --> 00:18:55,380
Bigger does not mean stronger.

262
00:18:55,381 --> 00:19:05,380
A medium-sized tanker compared to a little tugboat, the strength is about .2 horsepower to a ton of weight on a ship.

263
00:19:05,381 --> 00:19:12,520
on a ship and on our tugboats the way we calculate it it's about 14 horsepower to a ton of weight and

264
00:19:12,521 --> 00:19:19,420
that works out to about 70 to 1 difference a tugboat's very strong they're almost like a

265
00:19:19,421 --> 00:19:25,240
four-wheel drive in the water they pull real hard they don't slip a lot they don't go very fast but

266
00:19:25,241 --> 00:19:33,280
that's not what they were built for Foss maritime dolphin tugs are built for power and that means

267
00:19:33,281 --> 00:19:40,020
generating over 174,000 pounds of force to pull ships over a thousand times their size.

268
00:19:42,740 --> 00:19:46,360
A tugboat gets its strength not only from its engines and generators,

269
00:19:47,160 --> 00:19:51,780
but also two ASD, or azimuth stern drive propulsion drives.

270
00:19:54,500 --> 00:19:57,180
Each drive is basically a propeller and a nozzle,

271
00:19:57,181 --> 00:20:01,320
which has the ability to rotate 360 degrees.

272
00:20:03,280 --> 00:20:09,220
They're referred to as tractor tugs because they can maneuver the thrusts from the propellers

273
00:20:09,221 --> 00:20:12,500
in any direction.

274
00:20:12,501 --> 00:20:20,940
That means the operator can make the tug go any direction he wants, sideways, crossways,

275
00:20:20,941 --> 00:20:24,280
even in circles.

276
00:20:24,281 --> 00:20:28,820
That's extremely important when moving through the narrow channels of the port of Long Beach.

277
00:20:28,821 --> 00:20:33,680
The ship that we're going to be doing here is a smaller container ship.

278
00:20:35,780 --> 00:20:37,100
One on the bow, Roger.

279
00:20:37,900 --> 00:20:42,500
We'll pull his bow off the dock and he'll get underway.

280
00:20:44,060 --> 00:20:47,660
While Foss supplies tugs of all sizes to ports around the world,

281
00:20:48,500 --> 00:20:52,160
the Port of Long Beach is one of the only ports where you'll find one of their newest,

282
00:20:52,640 --> 00:20:55,180
strongest, and greenest tugboats.

283
00:20:55,181 --> 00:21:02,180
The Carolyn-Dorothy is the first and only hybrid system that's functioning in the tugboat world.

284
00:21:02,181 --> 00:21:09,280
Most of our day is spent running at about 10-15% as we move throughout the harbor, which

285
00:21:09,281 --> 00:21:10,436
is 80% of the time.

286
00:21:10,437 --> 00:21:15,180
Only 20% of the time we're working with ships that we need all our horsepower.

287
00:21:15,181 --> 00:21:23,180
The hybrid and its traditional sister dolphin tugs can all produce up to 5,080 horsepower.

288
00:21:23,181 --> 00:21:27,140
That's equivalent to the power of about 50 average four-door sedans.

289
00:21:29,140 --> 00:21:33,340
While they look a lot alike on the outside, the inside is another story.

290
00:21:35,100 --> 00:21:39,940
The hybrid tug has two main diesel engines that are 1,800 horsepower each,

291
00:21:40,580 --> 00:21:45,740
as well as two 440 horsepower generators and 10 tons of batteries.

292
00:21:46,740 --> 00:21:49,660
We can run totally green on batteries only.

293
00:21:49,940 --> 00:21:51,360
We can just add one generator.

294
00:21:51,361 --> 00:21:55,400
we can make about six and a half knots of speed doing that,

295
00:21:55,600 --> 00:21:58,320
and we can keep building it up by adding another engine.

296
00:21:59,360 --> 00:22:03,260
The tug continues to operate at that level until it reaches 90%.

297
00:22:03,261 --> 00:22:10,300
At that point, if we need more power, we can draw on the charged batteries so we get to 100%.

298
00:22:10,301 --> 00:22:16,700
We originally estimated about a 44% emission reduction and 20% to 30% fuel reduction.

299
00:22:16,701 --> 00:22:18,700
From the way that we've seen it work,

300
00:22:18,701 --> 00:22:21,700
we are pretty confident that it's going to be quite a bit higher.

301
00:22:21,701 --> 00:22:25,700
It works much better than we had originally calculated.

302
00:22:25,701 --> 00:22:28,700
You're at the cutting edge of technology for your industry.

303
00:22:28,701 --> 00:22:30,700
It's a lot of fun.

304
00:22:30,701 --> 00:22:34,700
Tugboats are all about big power in a little vessel.

305
00:22:34,701 --> 00:22:37,700
But it'll take a ship of super-titanic proportions

306
00:22:37,701 --> 00:22:42,700
to put more than 4,000 people at ease.

307
00:22:42,701 --> 00:22:45,700
With just over 300 ships in the fleet,

308
00:22:45,701 --> 00:22:53,700
LNG carriers still represent only a fraction of the more than 4,200 oil tankers currently traveling the world's oceans.

309
00:22:55,100 --> 00:22:57,940
Superships will return on Modern Marvels.

310
00:23:01,420 --> 00:23:06,700
When it comes to supersized ships, not many compare to large cruise ships.

311
00:23:08,040 --> 00:23:13,980
And in an ocean of these giants, Royal Caribbean's Freedom Class currently towers over them all.

312
00:23:14,780 --> 00:23:17,000
This class includes the Liberty of the Seas,

313
00:23:17,620 --> 00:23:19,620
docked at the port of Miami on the day we visited.

314
00:23:20,280 --> 00:23:24,300
Well, the size of Liberty of the Seas is really a pretty amazing number.

315
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:26,940
She's 160,000 gross registered tons.

316
00:23:28,400 --> 00:23:33,400
She's over 1,100 feet long, sits 207 feet above sea level,

317
00:23:33,800 --> 00:23:35,980
and is 185 feet wide.

318
00:23:37,040 --> 00:23:41,100
This mighty ship is three times heavier than the historic ocean colossus,

319
00:23:41,460 --> 00:23:42,160
the Titanic.

320
00:23:42,161 --> 00:23:46,480
It's also about 90 feet wider, 70 feet taller,

321
00:23:46,580 --> 00:23:49,280
and over 200 feet longer.

322
00:23:49,380 --> 00:23:53,220
And how would the Liberty of the Seas compare to the Mayflower?

323
00:23:53,320 --> 00:23:56,780
Roughly 36 Mayflowers could easily fit within the footprint

324
00:23:56,880 --> 00:23:59,960
of the Liberty's main deck.

325
00:24:00,060 --> 00:24:01,820
Her length is just about the height

326
00:24:01,920 --> 00:24:03,600
of the Empire State Building.

327
00:24:03,700 --> 00:24:05,500
She's huge.

328
00:24:05,600 --> 00:24:09,800
A huge ship means more guests and a larger crew.

329
00:24:09,801 --> 00:24:14,800
There's 3,600 guests on board, and there's just over 1,300 crew.

330
00:24:14,801 --> 00:24:17,800
So you really have a small city at sea.

331
00:24:17,801 --> 00:24:21,800
And the 1,300 crew are responsible for everything that you could think of.

332
00:24:21,801 --> 00:24:24,800
There's no vacation for anybody on board on the crew side.

333
00:24:24,801 --> 00:24:27,800
The Liberty of the Sea arrived this morning at about 6 o'clock,

334
00:24:27,801 --> 00:24:30,800
and she'll leave this afternoon at about 5.

335
00:24:30,801 --> 00:24:32,800
So the turnaround is immediate.

336
00:24:34,800 --> 00:24:37,800
Within about eight hours of that 11-hour window,

337
00:24:37,801 --> 00:24:40,680
the crew must transport luggage for 3,600,

338
00:24:42,580 --> 00:24:46,820
as well as enough supplies to feed nearly 5,000 people for a week.

339
00:24:48,920 --> 00:24:50,940
This is the aft portion of the ship

340
00:24:50,941 --> 00:24:53,500
where we have all our provisioning for food and beverage.

341
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:55,740
This is taking place today, right now.

342
00:24:56,160 --> 00:24:59,880
For sugar, it's approximately 6,000 pounds that we're taking,

343
00:25:00,280 --> 00:25:03,380
12,000 pounds of flour just for the seventh day.

344
00:25:03,381 --> 00:25:07,780
Poultry items, we have 15,000 pounds of poultry meat.

345
00:25:08,360 --> 00:25:14,500
It's almost 87,000 bottles of beer, including the cans that we take in every voyage.

346
00:25:15,100 --> 00:25:16,900
So it's quite a bit of work.

347
00:25:17,980 --> 00:25:18,680
Quite a bit.

348
00:25:20,100 --> 00:25:26,240
While the guests enjoy onboard luxuries, the crew on the bridge must maneuver the mammoth ship through the seas.

349
00:25:27,500 --> 00:25:31,160
The ship maneuvers like a yacht, I have to tell you.

350
00:25:31,320 --> 00:25:32,160
It is a big ship.

351
00:25:32,260 --> 00:25:32,960
It is heavy.

352
00:25:33,380 --> 00:25:40,100
but we have plenty of power and it has maneuverability is it's like you can turn it on a

353
00:25:40,101 --> 00:25:48,020
dime that's due in part because of the ship's two azipod propulsion systems allowing it to easily

354
00:25:48,021 --> 00:25:55,220
maneuver in any direction without the assistance of tugboats to go in and out of ports the bridge

355
00:25:55,221 --> 00:25:59,860
gains a little extra control from a good old-fashioned steering wheel you have the control

356
00:25:59,861 --> 00:26:05,380
right away here and we can give an order to the helmsman he can change the heading and change the

357
00:26:05,381 --> 00:26:12,180
rudder angle right away but before the ship leaves the dock a navigator pre-plans the route

358
00:26:13,060 --> 00:26:20,180
down to every turn he does this for redundancy we have it on the computer and we also have it on the

359
00:26:20,181 --> 00:26:26,340
paper chart so that's why we have the navigation officer he plans the route once the route is in

360
00:26:26,341 --> 00:26:30,620
in the computer, it's relatively easy sailing for the crew.

361
00:26:30,621 --> 00:26:32,640
When we are going between the ports,

362
00:26:32,641 --> 00:26:34,820
we are on, most of the time, autopilot.

363
00:26:34,821 --> 00:26:38,380
So it's automatic steering.

364
00:26:38,381 --> 00:26:40,120
Out in the ocean, this floating city

365
00:26:40,121 --> 00:26:43,160
can reach a cruising speed of roughly 22 knots,

366
00:26:43,161 --> 00:26:45,960
or 25 miles per hour.

367
00:26:45,961 --> 00:26:46,800
It's good.

368
00:26:46,801 --> 00:26:51,000
We can actually water ski behind if we like to.

369
00:26:51,001 --> 00:26:52,600
To keep this cruise ship cruising

370
00:26:52,601 --> 00:26:54,960
requires an impressive diesel-electric system.

371
00:26:57,260 --> 00:27:01,120
Six engines and generators supply up to 105,000 horsepower

372
00:27:01,121 --> 00:27:02,220
for the ship's propulsion,

373
00:27:03,560 --> 00:27:06,380
and as much as 75 kilowatts of electricity

374
00:27:06,381 --> 00:27:08,140
to power the rest of the ship.

375
00:27:09,220 --> 00:27:12,960
The same engines produce power for the whole ship

376
00:27:12,961 --> 00:27:16,320
and also for the motor that drives the ship forward.

377
00:27:17,040 --> 00:27:19,860
Basically, it's when we have 5,000 people on board.

378
00:27:19,861 --> 00:27:23,660
It is a small town and we have the same power plan

379
00:27:23,661 --> 00:27:26,660
which we will find on the shore for this town.

380
00:27:28,140 --> 00:27:30,240
From the engine room, the crew can oversee

381
00:27:30,241 --> 00:27:31,900
all of the ship's technical equipment,

382
00:27:32,520 --> 00:27:34,440
as well as its impressive water supply.

383
00:27:36,620 --> 00:27:39,500
Majority of the water we have on board that we drink

384
00:27:39,501 --> 00:27:42,660
and what we use for our swimming pools, shower,

385
00:27:43,200 --> 00:27:46,260
whatever is mainly produced on board.

386
00:27:46,261 --> 00:27:51,260
Two so-called evaporators and two reverse osmosis systems

387
00:27:51,261 --> 00:27:55,860
convert over 700,000 gallons of seawater to freshwater every day.

388
00:27:57,540 --> 00:28:01,500
So basically we can run without any freshwater from the shore.

389
00:28:03,160 --> 00:28:06,180
Some of that freshwater goes to the ship's three pools,

390
00:28:07,140 --> 00:28:09,820
six whirlpools, a small water park,

391
00:28:10,460 --> 00:28:12,920
a surfing simulator called the Flowrider,

392
00:28:13,340 --> 00:28:14,900
and an ice skating rink.

393
00:28:14,901 --> 00:28:16,260
Prefer to stay dry?

394
00:28:16,840 --> 00:28:19,400
This gigantic ship also has numerous restaurants,

395
00:28:20,420 --> 00:28:23,380
a full basketball court, miniature golf course,

396
00:28:23,740 --> 00:28:25,240
and a rock climbing wall.

397
00:28:28,700 --> 00:28:30,140
At the center of the ship,

398
00:28:30,260 --> 00:28:31,960
taking up nearly a third of its length,

399
00:28:32,780 --> 00:28:36,140
sits an enormous atrium called the Royal Promenade.

400
00:28:36,720 --> 00:28:39,800
It is so massive and so wide open,

401
00:28:39,980 --> 00:28:42,640
and you really feel like you're walking down this street

402
00:28:42,641 --> 00:28:46,180
in any city with the staterooms looking onto the street.

403
00:28:46,920 --> 00:28:49,180
While ships built with passenger comfort in mind

404
00:28:49,181 --> 00:28:51,260
had been sailing since the early 1800s,

405
00:28:52,520 --> 00:28:55,080
it wasn't until the late 1950s

406
00:28:55,081 --> 00:28:57,660
that the modern cruise ship industry came into being.

407
00:28:59,180 --> 00:29:01,920
It coincided with the emergence of the jet age.

408
00:29:02,900 --> 00:29:05,100
As passengers chose large, fast planes

409
00:29:05,101 --> 00:29:06,420
to get from point A to B,

410
00:29:07,420 --> 00:29:09,860
the cruise suddenly became more important

411
00:29:09,861 --> 00:29:10,860
than the destination.

412
00:29:12,640 --> 00:29:23,080
Royal Caribbean started in 1968, and from that point on, basically, Royal Caribbean has built evolutions of ships that have doubled in size every 10 years.

413
00:29:24,520 --> 00:29:32,120
To get cruise ships of this size out to sea involves at least two years of planning and design, and another two years of construction.

414
00:29:32,121 --> 00:29:36,580
Royal Caribbean's freedom ships may be super,

415
00:29:37,340 --> 00:29:40,400
but they'll soon be sharing the ocean with an even bigger class,

416
00:29:41,220 --> 00:29:44,000
carrying up to 5,400 passengers.

417
00:29:45,580 --> 00:29:48,240
Oasis of the Seas and her sister ship Allure of the Seas

418
00:29:48,241 --> 00:29:51,040
is going to be 220,000 gross registered tons.

419
00:29:51,280 --> 00:29:55,700
So it's the first time ever we're going to go over the 200,000 ton mark.

420
00:29:56,700 --> 00:29:58,800
Super-sized cruise ships are impressive,

421
00:29:58,801 --> 00:30:05,340
But they just don't have the capability to do many of the things this smaller Navy vessel can.

422
00:30:07,840 --> 00:30:15,580
In one week on the liberty of the seas, the ship's crew prepares over 100,000 meals and 90,000 desserts.

423
00:30:17,020 --> 00:30:19,580
Super ships will return on Modern Marvels.

424
00:30:22,620 --> 00:30:24,760
Different ships have different purposes.

425
00:30:24,761 --> 00:30:29,760
Container ships may specialize in transporting dry or refrigerated cargo,

426
00:30:29,761 --> 00:30:32,760
and tankers deliver fuel.

427
00:30:34,760 --> 00:30:37,760
But this multitasking ship does both.

428
00:30:38,760 --> 00:30:42,760
For the Navy, that's the role of the Carl Brashear.

429
00:30:42,761 --> 00:30:46,760
This is USNS Carl Brashear, named after the first black master diver in the Navy,

430
00:30:46,761 --> 00:30:50,760
also the first amputee ever to be reinstated into active duty.

431
00:30:54,760 --> 00:30:57,860
We're brand new, just fresh out of the box.

432
00:30:58,640 --> 00:30:59,340
The U.S.

433
00:30:59,341 --> 00:31:06,280
Navy's Military Sealift Command, or MSC, launched the Carl Brashear in September 2008.

434
00:31:07,340 --> 00:31:14,480
The ship is captained and crewed by 124 civilian mariners, with an 11-person military department for additional support.

435
00:31:15,500 --> 00:31:22,460
The Navy has turned over all of their logistics seagoing assets to Military Sealift Command.

436
00:31:22,461 --> 00:31:29,480
So we provide them with all of the ordnance, fuel, and food that they require to do their job.

437
00:31:31,200 --> 00:31:38,000
The ship is also part of MSC's TAKE fleet, their newest, most advanced replenishment ships.

438
00:31:39,220 --> 00:31:42,980
TAKEs in general can supply just about any ship out there,

439
00:31:43,120 --> 00:31:48,720
whether it's the U.S. Navy from an aircraft carrier to a frigate to a submarine

440
00:31:48,721 --> 00:31:53,760
or to a foreign military vessel, whatever the mission is,

441
00:31:53,880 --> 00:31:57,200
MSC will do the best to resupply their needs.

442
00:31:59,060 --> 00:32:01,440
Calling fuel and supplies is one thing,

443
00:32:02,080 --> 00:32:05,760
but where the Carl Brosheer excels is transferring it to another ship.

444
00:32:06,520 --> 00:32:08,920
Basically, if everything goes as planned,

445
00:32:09,020 --> 00:32:11,880
we should have the operation work almost like a symphony.

446
00:32:12,400 --> 00:32:14,540
Underway replenishment, or UNREP,

447
00:32:15,060 --> 00:32:17,080
involves moving cargo ship to ship

448
00:32:17,081 --> 00:32:20,060
while both are keeping a distance of about 200 feet

449
00:32:20,061 --> 00:32:23,720
and a constant speed of around 15 miles per hour.

450
00:32:24,580 --> 00:32:27,400
The first means to start the underway replenishment

451
00:32:27,401 --> 00:32:28,780
is by shooting the shot line.

452
00:32:29,140 --> 00:32:30,620
The shot line here is basically

453
00:32:30,621 --> 00:32:32,220
what gets the whole process started.

454
00:32:32,520 --> 00:32:34,240
This projectile to get set up

455
00:32:34,241 --> 00:32:37,640
is going to go into the end of this modified M14

456
00:32:37,641 --> 00:32:41,320
and the shot line will go in the holder here.

457
00:32:42,320 --> 00:32:44,740
On the cabin's word, we will blow the whistle one time.

458
00:32:45,120 --> 00:32:46,660
When they're ready, they'll blow it twice

459
00:32:46,661 --> 00:32:49,420
and we will fire the projectile over onto their deck.

460
00:32:53,600 --> 00:32:57,220
The crew ties the shot line to a heavy braided messenger line

461
00:32:57,221 --> 00:32:59,000
attached to a high-tension wire.

462
00:32:59,720 --> 00:33:01,680
The whole rig is dependent on the wire.

463
00:33:01,800 --> 00:33:04,660
They'll pull the wire across, and when they give us the word,

464
00:33:04,760 --> 00:33:06,280
we'll take a certain amount of tension,

465
00:33:06,400 --> 00:33:08,440
usually 11,000 pounds of tension on it.

466
00:33:08,640 --> 00:33:10,600
That makes everything nice and strong.

467
00:33:11,460 --> 00:33:15,020
That's strong enough to handle about 8,500 pounds of cargo

468
00:33:15,021 --> 00:33:16,320
or fuel at a time.

469
00:33:16,660 --> 00:33:23,660
For smaller loads of cargo, the ship can also use an onboard helicopter.

470
00:33:23,661 --> 00:33:30,660
This seagoing giant has 887,000 cubic feet of dry cargo space.

471
00:33:30,661 --> 00:33:35,660
That's equivalent to a football field almost two stories high.

472
00:33:35,661 --> 00:33:45,660
